                <h2>When to Use This Guide</h2>
<p>This guide applies when your Google Ads account has been suspended due to policy violations, payment issues, or suspicious activity, and you need to prepare an appeal to request reinstatement.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Access Your Account and Identify the Suspension Reason</h2>
<p>Log into your Google Ads dashboard and navigate to "Notifications" or "Account-level issues" to find the specific description of your suspension.</p>
<p><strong>Key information to note:</strong><br />
- Type of violation (brand policy, circumvention, misrepresentation, etc.)<br />
- Specific ads or content involved<br />
- Appeal deadline (if any)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If your dashboard only shows "Account suspended" without details, click the "Request review" link in your notification email, or visit the <a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/troubleshooter/4578507">Google Ads policy review page</a> directly.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Diagnose the Issue and Gather Evidence</h2>
<p>Prepare the following materials based on your suspension reason:</p>
<h3>General Checklist</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Document Type</th>
<th>What to Prepare</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Identity proof</td>
<td>Business license (digital copy), legal representative ID</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Website ownership</td>
<td>Domain DNS records, hosting account screenshots, Google Search Console verification</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Business permits</td>
<td>Industry-specific licenses (finance, healthcare, gaming approvals)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Ad screenshots</td>
<td>Full screenshots of disapproved ads and corrected versions</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Remediation statement</td>
<td>Specific corrective actions taken</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Additional Materials by Violation Type</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Trademark infringement</strong>: Provide trademark registration certificate or authorization documents</li>
<li><strong>Misleading claims</strong>: Product test reports, user reviews, or third-party certifications</li>
<li><strong>Inaccessible website</strong>: Screenshots showing the website is now functional</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Write Your Appeal Letter (The Critical Step)</h2>
<p>Your appeal letter determines your chances of success. Include these four sections:</p>
<h3>1. Introduction</h3>
<pre><code>Who we are: [Company Name], primarily engaged in [product/service description], operating [website URL].
Account ID: [Your Google Ads Customer ID]
</code></pre>
<h3>2. Acknowledge the Problem</h3>
<p><strong>Wrong approach:</strong> "Our ads are perfectly fine—this was a system error."</p>
<p><strong>Right approach:</strong> "We understand the suspension is due to [specific violation]. We did not have a thorough understanding of the relevant policy and take full responsibility."</p>
<h3>3. Detail Your Remediation</h3>
<ul>
<li>Deleted/modified ads: [List specific ad IDs or names]</li>
<li>Removed landing pages: [List specific page URLs]</li>
<li>Updated processes: [Describe internal review mechanisms]</li>
</ul>
<h3>4. Commit to Compliance</h3>
<pre><code>We commit to:
1. All ads will go through dual-review before launch
2. Monthly compliance audits going forward
3. Consulting Google's policy team proactively when uncertain
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Submit Your Appeal</h2>
<h3>Where to Submit</h3>
<ol>
<li>Through your dashboard: Help → Contact Us → Request Review</li>
<li>Or email Google Ads policy team directly (verify the correct email address)</li>
</ol>
<h3>Format Requirements</h3>
<ul>
<li>Submit appeal letter as plain text or PDF</li>
<li>Name attachments clearly (e.g., <code>business_license_2024.pdf</code>)</li>
<li>Email subject format: <code>Appeal for Account [CustomerID] - [Brief violation type]</code></li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Tip: Submit through both the dashboard form and email for double coverage.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Follow Up and Wait</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>First appeal response time</strong>: Usually 1-3 business days</li>
<li><strong>Complex cases</strong>: May extend to 5-7 business days</li>
<li><strong>Second appeal</strong>: If rejected, you may resubmit with new materials or more detailed remediation</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Follow-up tips:</strong><br />
- If no response after 5 business days, use dashboard Help → Chat to contact support<br />
- Keep your appeal email inbox active—Google may request additional materials</p>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Will my account balance be cleared during the appeal?</strong></p>
<p>No. The balance is frozen during suspension and will be restored immediately upon successful reinstatement.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Can I appeal multiple suspended accounts together?</strong></p>
<p>Submit separate appeals for each account, but you can note linked account IDs in each submission for Google's holistic review.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Can I appeal again if rejected?</strong></p>
<p>Yes. Wait at least 7 days before resubmitting, and ensure you've made substantive improvements.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>The core logic of account appeals is: <strong>Acknowledge → Prove remediation → Commit to compliance</strong>. The more complete your materials and the more sincere your explanation, the higher your chances of reinstatement. If you're struggling with a suspended account, start with Step 1 and work through the checklist.</p>
